This assert statement checks that the size of the ArrayList is always less than or equal to its capacity, which indicates that the internal array has been properly sized to hold all the elements in the ArrayList.

Here's the C# code block related to the assert statement:

```cs
public virtual int Capacity
{
    get
    {
        return _items.Length;
    }
    set
    {
        if (value < _size)
        {
            throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException("value", "SR.ArgumentOutOfRange_Capacity");
        }
        if (_items.Length != value)
        {
            Object[] newItems = new Object[value];
            _items.CopyTo(newItems, 0);
            _items = newItems;
            _version++;
        }
    }
}
```

The `Capacity` property gets or sets the capacity of the ArrayList. If the provided value is less than the current size of the ArrayList, an `ArgumentOutOfRangeException` is thrown. If the current length of the internal array does not match the provided value, a new array of the provided capacity is created and the old elements are copied to the new array. This ensures that the capacity of the ArrayList is always at least as large as the number of elements that it holds.
